# valgrind suppressions for scripts/leak-check.sh (#151). Each entry says why it is not a leak.

# Patterns use the demangled names, so that they match whichever symbol mangling the toolchain
# uses: the legacy scheme (`fuser::mnt::Mount::new`) and v0 (`<fuser::mnt::Mount>::new`, the
# default since the 1.9x toolchains). The earlier entry matched the legacy `_ZN...` names
# only, and stopped matching with a newer toolchain: the weekly run failed on a 55-byte,
# once-per-process block that was never a leak (#151).

# The mount of the binary's FUSE session (55 bytes in three blocks, once per process): the
# mountpoint path (fuser Mount), the mountpoint as a C string and the /dev/fuse handle
# (MountImpl). `serve` in src/main.rs keeps the mount in a ManuallyDrop on purpose, because
# its drop would unmount by path, and the path may hold a newer sacdfs's mount by then (#51).
# All three blocks are allocated inside Mount::new, called from Session::new in sacdfs::run:
# once per process, no growth.
{
   sacdfs-session-mount-kept-on-purpose
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:*fuser*mnt*Mount*new
   ...
   fun:*sacdfs*run
}

# fuser 0.18.0 (fuse_pure.rs, receive_fusermount_message) reads fusermount's reply with nix
# 0.31's recvmsg::<SockaddrStorage>. The reply comes over a connected socketpair, so the
# kernel fills in no sender address (msg_namelen is 0), and nix still reads the family out of
# the uninitialised SockaddrStorage in set_length (addr.rs), whose result nix then discards
# (`let _ = address.set_length(..)`). A read in nix of a value nobody uses: not sacdfs's,
# once per mount, not a leak.
{
   nix-recvmsg-sockaddr-storage-set-length-unused
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*SockaddrStorage*set_length
   ...
   fun:*receive_fusermount_message
}
